# Level 11 → 12

## Goal
Find the password inside `data.txt`, which is encoded using ROT13 (each letter shifted 13 places 
in the alphabet).

## Commands used
```bash
ssh bandit11@bandit.labs.overthewire.org -p 2220
cat data.txt | tr 'A-Za-z' 'N-ZA-Mn-za-m'
```

## Explanation
ROT13 is a simple substitution cipher that shifts each letter 13 positions through the alphabet 
(wrapping around after Z). The `tr` command maps the normal alphabet (`A-Za-z`) to its ROT13-shifted 
equivalent (`N-ZA-Mn-za-m`), decoding the text back to readable form. Since 13 is exactly half of 26, 
applying ROT13 twice returns the original text — so encoding and decoding use the identical command.

